758:    The performance of the algorithm is given by the number of constraints in $C / {\sim}$ as a function of $n = |X|$.
760:We call the equivalence relation $\sim$ outputted by a strong sparsification algorithm a \emph{strong sparsifier}.
762:In this section, we study the \emph{mere existence} of strong sparsifiers for classic computational problems that can be phrased as CSPs. In particular, we will give combinatorial lower bounds for strong sparsification for monotone 1-in-3-SAT: there exist instances with $n$ vertices and $\Omega(n^{1.725\cdots})$ clauses where every strong sparsifier is trivial, i.e.~merges no pair of vertices.
